# Line endings, pinned per file type.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is set globally on this machine, which means a fresh
# checkout rewrites LF files to CRLF on Windows. That is harmless for source code
# and catastrophic for the generated print assets: `npm run test:qr` compares the
# committed SVG against what the generator produces BYTE FOR BYTE, so a checkout
# that helpfully converts the line endings fails a check on a file that is
# actually correct. The failure would point at the QR rather than at the settings.
#
# Marking these `-text` says: do not touch the bytes. The generator writes LF and
# means it.

# The attendee-portal QR. XML is perfectly valid with either ending, so the only
# reason to care is the byte-for-byte verification above.
print/*.svg -text

# The raster is already binary; make sure no tool tries to line-ending "fix" it.
print/*.png binary